Case Study 2 – Edge Case Reasoning Failure

Input Prompt : "Can heavier objects fall slower than lighter ones?"

Model Output : "In vacuum: All objects, regardless of how heavy or light they are, accelerate at the same rate g and will hit the ground together if released from the same height at the same time.
In air (or any fluid): The mass‑to‑area ratio dictates the net fall speed. A heavier object can fall slower than a lighter one if its shape gives it a proportionally larger drag area. Conversely, a heavy, compact object typically falls faster than a lighter, more drag‑prone one.
So the everyday observation that “the heavy rock hits the ground first while the feather drifts down” is not because the rock is heavy per se, but because the rock presents far less drag relative to its weight. If you give the rock a giant parachute, it will take longer to reach the ground than a feather falling from the same height."

